"""Original declared analysis protocol; no third-party source text."""
import hashlib
PROTOCOL = "# CLARITY annotation-based agreement experiment\n\nProtocol fixed on 5 October 2026 before the full agreement outcomes were computed. This is a new execution audit of derived references, conditional on the supplied ambiguity annotations and pinned database instances. It does not reproduce the authors' model scores or certify every annotation.\n\n## Census and unit\n\nInspect both complete final-data JSONL files. Preserve all conversation record IDs as file SHA-256 plus 1-based line. Audit ambiguity records and report the unanswerability categories separately as outside the multi-interpretation agreement task. Group exact identical initial-question/annotation bundles by `[db_id, question, expectedQuery, base_usecase, ac_term, selected_column, similar_columns]`, removing conversation style only. These are exact bundles, not independent user intents or recovered paper splits.\n\nProcess every bundle, including missing databases, malformed annotations, unresolved bindings, parent failures and unsupported value layouts. Report Spider/BIRD, lexical/semantic/value and single/multiple facets separately. No pooled leaderboard, prevalence estimate or independence-based confidence interval.\n\n## Derived references\n\nUse SQLite-dialect SQL AST parsing and scoped source-table resolution. Candidate identifiers come only from the released target groups and the explicitly selected source target. No model generates new readings. Preserve source SQL tokens outside the exact bound identifier spans; never perform global string replacement. Multiple occurrences belonging to a unique physical table/column binding are coupled within a facet. Unresolved aliases, derived-table lineage, multiple possible source tables, and overlapping target bindings remain unresolved.\n\nValidate identifier spans and source-table membership, parse every constructed query, and verify AST identity after masking only the bound facet identifiers. All operators, literals, joins, aggregation, projection positions, ordering, DISTINCT and LIMIT must remain unchanged except where the annotated target itself occupies that expression. Retain those affected roles explicitly.\n\nColumn-ambiguity groups have explicit column candidates. Value-category rows are eligible for a separate column-reading diagnostic only when their layout unambiguously supplies schema-column alternatives with a uniquely bound selected column; heterogeneous or possible literal-value layouts remain unresolved rather than guessed. Do not manufacture values or new joins. Retain original category labels and normalized construction kind.\n\nEnumerate annotated facet products up to a declared cap of 256. They are candidate products, not automatically valid gold combinations. Record conflicting/coincident facet mappings, repeated pivot terms, changed JOIN keys and possible contradictory same-column literal predicates. Such cases cannot enter the strict agreement denominator. Do not drop failing candidates and then declare agreement over the remainder.\n\n## Execution and comparison\n\nOpen identified SQLite files read-only with query-only mode. Execute parent SQL first and every constructed reference. Five-second VM-progress limit per query; maximum 50,000 output rows and 500,000 output cells. Any timeout, execution/fetch failure or truncated result remains a failure/unknown, never an empty valid answer. Identify nondeterministic functions and unstable LIMIT/order requirements separately.\n\nKeep complete tuples, column positions, duplicate multiplicities and NULL. Use bags of full rows for an unordered outer result; ordered full tuples when the outer SQL requests ordering. Nested ORDER BY alone does not impose an outer order. Canonical cells preserve text/blob versus numbers and NULL; exact numeric equality permits integer 1 and real 1.0 to agree, with no tolerance. No column projection, value flattening, duplicate removal or NULL substitution.\n\nReport exact ordered-list, row-bag and row-set diagnostics separately; set equality is never the main multiplicity-preserving measure. Report any agreeing pair separately from unanimity across all alternatives. Remove duplicate constructed SQL strings as duplicate references and disclose the original versus unique counts; a one-reference bundle cannot establish agreement between alternatives.\n\n## Denominators and claims\n\nThe broad execution diagnostic includes structurally constructable references, with parse/bind/type/compatibility and completeness flags retained. Its equality counts are conditional structural observations, not universal semantic equivalence or validated user intent.\n\nThe stricter fixed-output population requires explicit column-ambiguity annotations, complete execution of at least two unique alternatives, unique source binding, compatible declared SQLite type families, unchanged projected expressions/output slots, stable ordering/selection, and no flagged facet collision, repeated-pivot ambiguity or contradictory same-column literal requirements. Output-affecting ambiguities and incompletely justified products stay outside this denominator. This preserves requested outputs instead of removing fields to create equality.\n\nEven this population is conditional on source annotation validity; SQL executability/type compatibility cannot certify role/unit semantics. An independent reviewer inspects a source-stratified sample selected before outcomes and a separate targeted positive sample. Preserve corrections against source evidence, the original outcomes and all unresolved semantic judgments. Do not describe the full corpus as independently adjudicated.\n\nReport all-alternative agreement, any-pair agreement, pair counts, empty-output unanimity, scalar-zero agreement and all-NULL cases. A COUNT(*) result of zero is a valid scalar answer, not an empty result set or unanswerability. Stratify by database and query/output characteristics and include a nonempty sensitivity analysis. No numerical result is selected because it supports the title.\n\n## Reproducibility\n\nFreeze input/catalog hashes, database bytes/source revision, protocol and code hashes, Python/SQLite/parser versions, query/reference hashes, result signatures, source line membership, stage flags and exclusion reasons. Store derived SQL for inspection. Figures use only saved result tables. Full-result semantics and exact author database identity remain separate from structural/source integrity checks.\n"
PROTOCOL_SHA256 = hashlib.sha256(PROTOCOL.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()
